---
import ArticleLayout from "@/layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Why I’m building Mimic"
  eyebrow="About · moreveal"
  icon="user"
  description="Browser automation should spend its time on the work that matters."
>
  <div class="story">
    <p class="lead">
      I’m Slava. After years of browser automation, I kept running into the same
      choice: make everything work over HTTP, or pay for an entire browser.
    </p>
    <h2>The gap I kept coming back to</h2>
    <p>
      HTTP is fast and inexpensive. But on complex websites, the missing browser
      behavior becomes your problem: client-side scripts, state, navigation and
      the observations a site expects. Eventually, reproducing that behavior
      takes more effort than the automation itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Chromium handles much of that work for you. It also brings a rendering
      stack to jobs that may only need JavaScript and a few values from the DOM.
      I wanted something between those extremes.
    </p>
    <h2>That question became Mimic</h2>
    <p>
      I started building a browser execution runtime in Go around V8. The
      browser environment lives in Mimic: DOM, networking, frames, Workers and
      observable state. CDP lets familiar automation tools connect to it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The aim is to bring execution time and resource costs closer to HTTP while
      preserving the browser behavior the workload depends on, including
      consistent observations used in fingerprinting. It is an engineering goal,
      not a promise that every workflow already works.
    </p>
    <h2>Compatibility through evidence</h2>
    <p>
      I compare behavior against Chrome 152 and fix the underlying semantics
      when execution diverges. Real workloads are how I learn which missing
      capabilities matter. Mimic is still a public beta; some workloads favor
      Chrome, and compatibility remains incomplete.
    </p>
    <h2>Bring the workflow that breaks it</h2>
    <p>
      If browser startup, memory or concurrency is getting in your way, I’d like
      to understand your case. I’m happy to work with early users on integration
      and compatibility rather than guess what they need.
    </p>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="button button-primary" href="https://t.me/moreveal"
        >Talk about your workload</a
      ><a class="button" href={`${base}download/`}>Try Mimic</a>
    </div>
    <div class="story-sources">
      <h3>From the development journal</h3><p>
        <a
          href="https://www.reddit.com/r/WebScrapingInsider/comments/1wfh7dv/i_got_tired_of_choosing_between_raw_http_and/"
          >Why I started building something between HTTP and Chromium</a
        > · September 13, 2026
      </p><p>
        <a
          href="https://www.reddit.com/r/webscraping/comments/1wowgnq/were_doing_web_scraping_wrong/"
          >Building a runtime around the automation workload</a
        > · September 24, 2026
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</ArticleLayout>
<style>
  .story {
    max-width: 1020px;
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: 24px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .story-sources {
    padding: 24px;
    margin-top: 40px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 12px;
    background: var(--surface);
    font-size: 14px;
  }
  .story-sources h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  @media (min-width: 1500px) {
    .lead {
      font-size: 30px;
    }
    .story {
      max-width: 1200px;
    }
  }
</style>
